# Step 4 — Add `IronPdfAiInitializer` to `KnowledgeOps.AI`

Create:

```text
KnowledgeOps.AI/IronPdfAiInitializer.cs
```

```csharp
using IronPdf.AI;
using Microsoft.Extensions.AI;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Embeddings;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Memory;

namespace KnowledgeOps.AI;

public sealed class IronPdfAiInitializer(
    Kernel kernel,
    IEmbeddingGenerator<string, Embedding<float>> embeddingGenerator,
    ILogger<IronPdfAiInitializer> logger)
{
    private int _initialized;

    public void EnsureInitialized()
    {
        if (Interlocked.Exchange(ref _initialized, 1) == 1)
        {
            return;
        }

        var embeddingService =
            embeddingGenerator.AsTextEmbeddingGenerationService();

        var memory = new SemanticTextMemory(
            new VolatileMemoryStore(),
            embeddingService);

        IronDocumentAI.Initialize(kernel, memory);

        logger.LogInformation(
            "IronPDF AI initialized with the shared KnowledgeOps Semantic Kernel.");
    }
}
```

## Why this is the safe implementation

This avoids the method that caused the compile error:

```csharp
WithAzureOpenAITextEmbeddingGeneration(...)
```

Instead, it reuses the already centralized `Microsoft.Extensions.AI` embedding generator.

Microsoft documents `AsTextEmbeddingGenerationService(...)` as a bridge from `IEmbeddingGenerator<string, Embedding<float>>` into Semantic Kernel’s `ITextEmbeddingGenerationService`, and Microsoft’s migration guidance identifies `IEmbeddingGenerator<string, Embedding<float>>` as the newer preferred embedding abstraction. ([Microsoft Learn][2])

## Instructor explanation

Say:

> IronPDF AI still expects Semantic Kernel memory during initialization. Rather than creating another Azure OpenAI embedding setup, we adapt the embedding generator already registered by the application. This keeps the Azure OpenAI configuration centralized and avoids duplicated setup.

