# Instructor narration script

## Opening

> In the previous cleanup, we centralized AI service registration so the Web project no longer creates Azure OpenAI clients directly. Now we can use that cleaner foundation to add IronPDF AI summarization to the existing document-processing pipeline.

## Explain the problem

> Right now, the document preview is just the first part of the extracted text. That is useful, but it is not a true summary. A summary should tell the user what the document is about.

## Explain the design

> We will keep the processing flow where it is. The only behavior change is inside the preview creation step. We will try to summarize the PDF using IronPDF AI. If that fails, we fall back to the original text preview.

## Explain the initializer

> IronPDF AI needs Semantic Kernel and memory to be initialized. We keep that setup inside `KnowledgeOps.AI`, because that project already owns our AI configuration. The Web project only asks the initializer to make sure IronPDF AI is ready.

## Explain the summarization call

> Once IronPDF AI is initialized, the actual summary call is simple: `await pdf.Summarize()`. IronPDF handles the PDF-to-AI preparation internally.

## Closing

> This gives users a more useful document preview without changing the UI, database, or workflow. The system still extracts and stores raw text for RAG, but the preview now becomes a proper AI-generated summary.

